# tools/ales_multitap_time_witness.rish — the multi-tap delay in real time: taps named in milliseconds (ALES69).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES69.
# Design read: active-designing/20260814-193359_fill-ales69-lotus-multi-tap-delay-in-real-time.md
# Stands on: lotus/taps.rye (ALES68 — the multi-tap delay this speaks in real time, delegated unchanged); lotus/clock.rye (ALES5 — the
# sample clock, its samples_for converting ms to a bounded sample count); lotus/echo_time.rye (ALES67 — the timed twin this mirrors).
#
# lotus/multitap_time.rye gives the ALES68 multi-tap a face in milliseconds, mirroring echo_ms over the feedback echo. multitap_ms
# converts start_ms, count_ms, and each tap's delay_ms through ALES5's proven clock.samples_for into an index-named Tap set and delegates
# to ALES68's multitap UNCHANGED, so the millisecond-named multi-tap lands BYTE-FOR-BYTE the same samples as the index-named multi-tap
# over the converted delays and span. The selftest proves: the clock adds only units (both tools run, the exact two independent copies
# 8000 dry / 4000 at 2 / 2000 at 4); at 48 kHz 1 ms converts to 48 samples and the copy sits exactly there; the two timed taps stay
# independent (the 4 ms copy 1/4 of the ORIGINAL dry, not a copy-of-a-copy); a sub-sample tap (0 ms) refuses BadDelay rather than silently
# doing nothing; a delay past the clip refuses DurationTooLong; zero taps refuse BadTapCount, a tap above unity BadLevel, an out-of-range
# span BadRange. Purely local — the clock a samples-per-second count, no lookahead, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/ales_multitap_time_witness.rish

say "ales-multitap-time: Language EN — ALES68 opened the multi-tap delay but named each tap's delay in sample INDICES. A musician names an early reflection in TIME — a tap a tenth of a second later — and the one missing fact is the sample rate, which ALES5's validated Clock holds. Every index-named rung has earned its real-time twin this way: fade_ms over the fade, place_at, marker_time over the markers, and one rung ago echo_ms over the feedback echo. This rung gives the multi-tap the same face, mirroring echo_ms exactly."
say "ales-multitap-time: Style Radiant — the law is stated positively: the CLOCK ADDS ONLY THE UNITS, NEVER THE AUDIO. multitap_ms converts start_ms, count_ms, and each tap's delay_ms through ALES5's samples_for and delegates to ALES68's multitap unchanged, so the millisecond-named multi-tap lands byte-for-byte the same samples as the index-named multi-tap over the converted delays and span; a sub-sample tap converts to zero and refuses BadDelay honestly, rather than silently doing nothing."
say "ales-multitap-time: Lens TAME — one clip of i16 PCM, a validated clock, a bounded slice of at most max_taps timed taps each a u32 delay_ms and a plain u32/u32 level; the count is checked before any conversion, each delay converted into a bounded stack Tap buffer, then the proven multi-tap run; DurationTooLong from the clock and BadTapCount / BadDelay / BadLevel / BadRange from the multi-tap forward by name before any write, the clip untouched on refusal; siloed to lotus/, no new arithmetic on the audio path, no lookahead, no network, no keys, no funds, no device."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/multitap_time"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/multitap_time.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-multitap-time: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/multitap_time.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-multitap-time: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-multitap-time: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-multitap-time' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-multitap-time: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-multitap-time line"

# This rung delegates to ALES68's multitap over its public API — re-prove the index-named multi-tap still stands green beside it.
let taps = run ["sh" "-c" "env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/taps.rye -femit-bin=lotus/bin/taps && lotus/bin/taps selftest 2>&1 | grep -q 'GREEN ales-taps' && echo yes"]
assert (trim taps.out) == "yes" else "ales-multitap-time: the ALES68 multi-tap delay regressed under its real-time twin"

say "ales-multitap-time: GREEN — the multi-tap delay in real time: taps named in milliseconds, a thin twin over ALES68 exactly as echo_ms is over the feedback echo. The clock adds only the units, never the audio: multitap_ms converts start_ms, count_ms, and each tap's delay_ms through ALES5's samples_for and delegates to ALES68's multitap unchanged, so the millisecond-named multi-tap lands byte-for-byte the same samples as the index-named multi-tap (the exact two independent copies 8000 dry / 4000 at 2 / 2000 at 4); at 48 kHz 1 ms converts to 48 samples and the copy sits exactly there; the two timed taps stay independent (the 4 ms copy 1/4 of the ORIGINAL dry); a sub-sample tap refuses BadDelay, a delay past the clip DurationTooLong, zero taps BadTapCount, a tap above unity BadLevel, an out-of-range span BadRange, each leaving the clip untouched; the ALES68 multi-tap stays GREEN beside it. The modulated delay (chorus, flanger — the moving tap wants fractional-delay interpolation) is the next genuinely new primitive, nameable in milliseconds from here."
